WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.840
A normal hearing test does not mean the cochlea is intact.

2
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.820
In mice, hearing thresholds recovered after loud noise.

3
00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:16.300
Yet about half the ribbon synapses, the contacts that pass sound from hair cells to

4
00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:20.100
the auditory nerve, were lost at one place along the cochlea.

5
00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:24.060
A threshold tells us the quietest sound that gets a response.

6
00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:27.200
It does not count the synapses carrying the rest.

7
00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:31.640
That matters because recovered sensitivity can conceal injury.

8
00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:37.560
Repair then poses different problems: restoring release in surviving cells, or

9
00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.220
replacing hair cells that are lost.

10
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:44.220
Kujawa and Liberman showed this in male CBA/CaJ mice.

11
00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:48.740
By two weeks after noise, thresholds were back to their old values.

12
00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:50.960
Hair cells remained intact.

13
00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:56.640
At eight weeks, ribbon counts at the 32 kHz place were about half of control.

14
00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:59.540
The cochlear nerve degenerated later.

15
00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:05.360
Human temporal bones also show auditory nerve fibre loss, worse with noise.

16
00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.540
But no one can count synapses in a living ear.

17
00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:12.820
Physiological proxies have uncertain sensitivity in people.

18
00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:16.880
A listening complaint alone does not establish the lesion.

19
00:01:17.833 --> 00:01:23.053
Gene replacement has improved hearing in children with OTOF-related deafness.

20
00:01:23.913 --> 00:01:26.473
The missing activity sits in cells that survive.

21
00:01:27.453 --> 00:01:31.373
The otoferlin gene is too large for one conventional AAV vector.

22
00:01:32.293 --> 00:01:36.833
In mice, researchers split it across two vectors so cells receiving both could

23
00:01:36.833 --> 00:01:40.573
rebuild it. Human trials test hearing recovery separately.

24
00:01:40.573 --> 00:01:48.193
In the DB-OTO trial, 9 of 12 children met the week-24 endpoint: an average threshold

25
00:01:48.193 --> 00:01:50.493
of 70 dB HL or better.

26
00:01:51.373 --> 00:01:53.473
Three reached average normal sensitivity.

27
00:01:54.493 --> 00:01:56.813
This treats OTOF-related deafness.

28
00:01:57.713 --> 00:01:59.493
Lifelong benefit remains unproven.

29
00:02:00.567 --> 00:02:03.447
Birds show that hair-cell loss need not be permanent.

30
00:02:04.247 --> 00:02:05.987
They regrow auditory hair cells.

31
00:02:06.907 --> 00:02:10.067
Fish regrow hair cells in the lateral line, a different organ.

32
00:02:11.027 --> 00:02:15.047
Mature mammalian cochleas lack comparable spontaneous replacement.

33
00:02:16.067 --> 00:02:20.867
Mammals do have a vestibular exception: hair cells reappeared in guinea-pig utricles

34
00:02:20.867 --> 00:02:24.467
after damage. That is a different organ from the cochlea.

35
00:02:25.307 --> 00:02:29.527
Replacing lost cochlear hair cells remains an experimental repair goal.

36
00:02:30.517 --> 00:02:32.337
What must a repaired synapse do?

37
00:02:33.157 --> 00:02:36.997
An inner hair cell releases transmitter onto auditory nerve fibres.

38
00:02:37.997 --> 00:02:40.177
Its ribbon helps keep vesicles in supply.

39
00:02:41.257 --> 00:02:42.817
Otoferlin is essential for release.

40
00:02:43.757 --> 00:02:46.757
In mice lacking it, exocytosis was almost abolished.

41
00:02:47.737 --> 00:02:51.797
Changing its calcium binding reduced the calcium sensitivity of release.

42
00:02:52.637 --> 00:02:56.717
That supports a calcium-sensing role, without showing it is the only sensor.
